{"builtWith":"0.15.16","comments":[{"LineComment":" | Canary for a known limitation of the native-loop lowering (#233)."},{"LineComment":" |"},{"LineComment":" | Recognition matches a combinator head in two forms — a direct"},{"LineComment":" | imported reference and the dissolved foreign-accessor read — but"},{"LineComment":" | does not resolve a top-level alias, the way magic-do's chain-head"},{"LineComment":" | recognition does (`isCanonicalHead` resolves one hop). The"},{"LineComment":" | optimizer normally dissolves such an alias before code generation,"},{"LineComment":" | so the gap is unreachable on ordinary code; the `inline never`"},{"LineComment":" | directive in `directives.txt` next to these goldens pins the alias"},{"LineComment":" | undissolved and exposes it."},{"LineComment":" |"},{"LineComment":" | `golden.lua` therefore pins the UNLOWERED shape — the foreign call"},{"LineComment":" | `myFor(1)(3)(f)()`. That is the current limitation, not the target."},{"LineComment":" | When recognition moves into the IR (#239 needs the loop visible to"},{"LineComment":" | the optimizer anyway), this golden should flip to a native `for`,"},{"LineComment":" | and that flip is the proof the move closed the gap."},{"LineComment":" |"},{"LineComment":" | Semantics are unaffected either way — the foreign combinator and"},{"LineComment":" | the emitted loop iterate identically — so `eval/golden.txt` holds"},{"LineComment":" | across the flip and only `golden.lua` discriminates."},{"LineComment":" |"},{"LineComment":" | `Golden.NativeLoops.Test` covers the same alias without the"},{"LineComment":" | directive, where the lowering does fire."}],"decls":[{"annotation":{"meta":null,"sourceSpan":{"end":[0,0],"start":[0,0]}},"bindType":"NonRec","expression":{"abstraction":{"abstraction":{"annotation":{"meta":{"metaType":"IsForeign"},"sourceSpan":{"end":[38,15],"start":[38,3]}},"type":"Var","value":{"identifier":"discard","moduleName":["Control","Bind"]}},"annotation":{"meta":